WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Film Courage: What are the steps to&nbsp;
developing and building a character?

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:10.160
Robert: The steps to developing and building a&nbsp;
character I have found has evolved greatly for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:18.240
me but first it was to sort of tap into my own&nbsp;
subconscious of just how do I see this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.600
I'm super visual and so if a character comes&nbsp;
out I can see that person in all detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.200
and so it's first to like&nbsp;
understand what that vision is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.920
and then second it's to ask myself who do&nbsp;
I know in the real world that's like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.560
and to use that as a sort of reflection of&nbsp;
of what I see versus what's in the real world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:54.080
and and then also to compare it to other movies&nbsp;
how has how is a character like this shown up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.240
in other films and what's worked and what hasn't&nbsp;
and also to ask what is the role of this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:07.680
in the story and and it's it's all of those but&nbsp;
but primarily it's to draw it back to reality is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.880
and not just not just reality but also&nbsp;
to put it in your own frame of mind is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.400
if I was in this position what would I do or if my&nbsp;
very best friend who's very akin to this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.400
was in this situation what would they do and I'm&nbsp;
also just a strong proponent of of speaking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.840
those people right like if you have a character&nbsp;
that's that's a bit of a match with with somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.160
hanging out with them picking up on their&nbsp;
eccentricities and the ways that they say things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.800
and and maybe even calling them up and saying&nbsp;
look if somebody said this to you what would your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.920
response be you should get a release if you have&nbsp;
them literally providing lines for your script but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:00.880
but those types of things I found is that&nbsp;
you just get so much nuance and and unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.840
dimension by following those steps that&nbsp;
it's something I try to always employ

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.040
here's what the screenwriter of Strive Sha-Risse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.920
Smith said about you I don't know how it's&nbsp;
possible but physically and emotionally Robert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.560
understands what Kalani the main female&nbsp;
protagonist in Strive is going through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:31.200
and that is what's important so can you&nbsp;
explain how you have empathy for characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.400
so in reading scripts I always ask myself&nbsp;
is this a story that I know and what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:48.000
ironic about Strive is that or I guess different&nbsp;
about strive is that it was set in Harlem and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.280
a world that on the surface I shouldn't&nbsp;
understand and isn't my story that said&nbsp;&nbsp;

